# AutoML SaaS Engine

### A reusable AutoML pipeline for tabular classification and regression

Automatically inspect datasets, preprocess features, compare models, tune hyperparameters, evaluate performance, and export deployable ML artifacts.

[![Python](https://img.shields.io/badge/Python-3.10%2B-blue?logo=python&logoColor=white)](https://www.python.org/)
[![scikit-learn](https://img.shields.io/badge/scikit--learn-ML-orange?logo=scikitlearn&logoColor=white)](https://scikit-learn.org/)
[![XGBoost](https://img.shields.io/badge/XGBoost-Enabled-red)](https://xgboost.readthedocs.io/)
[![Jupyter](https://img.shields.io/badge/Jupyter-Notebook-orange?logo=jupyter&logoColor=white)](https://jupyter.org/)
[![License](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-green.svg)](LICENSE)

</div>

---

## Overview

**AutoML SaaS Engine** is a reusable machine-learning workflow for tabular datasets.

A user provides:

- A CSV dataset
- The target column
- An optional task type
- A tuning mode

The engine then automatically:

1. Validates and inspects the dataset
2. Cleans duplicate and invalid records
3. Detects classification or regression
4. Identifies numerical and categorical features
5. Builds a leakage-safe preprocessing pipeline
6. Compares multiple machine-learning models
7. Performs cross-validation
8. Tunes the strongest models
9. Selects and evaluates the final model
10. Exports the complete trained pipeline and experiment artifacts

The current implementation is the **machine-learning engine for Phase 1** of a future SaaS platform.

---

## Why This Project?

Building a machine-learning model in a notebook is straightforward.

Building a reusable system that can work with different datasets requires:

- Automated data validation
- Reusable preprocessing
- Task-aware model selection
- Cross-validation
- Hyperparameter optimization
- Failure handling
- Experiment tracking
- Model serialization
- Reproducible outputs

This project focuses on those production-oriented ML engineering concepts.

---

## Workflow

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Upload CSV] --> B[Validate Dataset]
    B --> C[Inspect Data Quality]
    C --> D[Clean Dataset]
    D --> E[Detect Task Type]
    E --> F[Detect Feature Types]
    F --> G[Build Preprocessing Pipeline]
    G --> H[Train Baseline Models]
    H --> I[Cross-Validation Leaderboard]
    I --> J[Select Top Models]
    J --> K[Hyperparameter Tuning]
    K --> L[Select Final Model]
    L --> M[Evaluate on Test Set]
    M --> N[Export Model and Reports]
```

---

## Features

### Dataset handling

- CSV validation
- Empty-dataset detection
- Duplicate-column detection
- Duplicate-row removal
- Missing-target removal
- Constant-column removal
- Optional manual column removal
- Possible identifier-column detection
- Column-level data-quality reporting

### Automatic ML preparation

- Classification and regression detection
- Target-label encoding
- Numerical and categorical feature detection
- Median imputation for numerical features
- Most-frequent imputation for categorical features
- Numerical feature scaling
- One-hot encoding
- Unknown-category handling

### Model evaluation

- Reproducible train/test splitting
- Stratified K-Fold for classification
- K-Fold for regression
- Multiple evaluation metrics
- Model stability measurement
- Failure-tolerant model evaluation
- Dummy baselines for comparison

### Hyperparameter optimization

- Automatic selection of top-performing models
- `RandomizedSearchCV`
- Fast, balanced, and thorough tuning modes
- Model-specific search spaces
- Baseline fallback when tuning fails

### Artifact export

- Complete preprocessing and model pipeline
- Target encoder
- Input schema
- Model metadata
- Test metrics
- Baseline leaderboard
- Tuned leaderboard
- Classification report
- Prediction preview
- Failure logs

---

## Supported Models

### Classification

| Model | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Dummy Classifier | Majority-class baseline |
| Logistic Regression | Linear classification baseline |
| K-Nearest Neighbors | Distance-based classification |
| Random Forest | Bagging-based tree ensemble |
| Extra Trees | Highly randomized tree ensemble |
| XGBoost | Gradient-boosted decision trees |

### Regression

| Model | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Dummy Regressor | Mean-prediction baseline |
| Ridge Regression | Regularized linear regression |
| K-Nearest Neighbors | Distance-based regression |
| Random Forest | Bagging-based tree ensemble |
| Extra Trees | Highly randomized tree ensemble |
| XGBoost | Gradient-boosted decision trees |

---

## Evaluation Metrics

### Classification

- Accuracy
- Balanced accuracy
- Macro precision
- Macro recall
- Macro F1
- ROC-AUC
- Per-class classification report
- Confusion matrix

**Macro F1** is used as the primary classification metric because it gives equal importance to every class.

### Regression

- Mean Absolute Error
- Root Mean Squared Error
- R² score

**RMSE** is currently used as the primary regression model-selection metric.

---

## Example Usage

```python
automl_result = run_automl(
    csv_path="data/insurance.csv",
    target_column="charges",
    task="auto",
    tuning_mode="fast"
)
```

The engine returns:

```python
{
    "task_type": ...,
    "final_model_name": ...,
    "final_pipeline": ...,
    "final_cv_score": ...,
    "final_parameters": ...,
    "final_evaluation": ...,
    "baseline_leaderboard": ...,
    "tuned_leaderboard": ...,
    "target_encoder": ...,
    "artifact_paths": ...,
    "dataset_summary": ...,
    "cleaning_actions": ...
}
```

### Available tuning modes

```python
tuning_mode="fast"
```

- Tunes one model
- Uses fewer search iterations
- Best for quick testing

```python
tuning_mode="balanced"
```

- Tunes the two strongest models
- Balances training time and performance

```python
tuning_mode="thorough"
```

- Tunes more models
- Uses a larger search budget
- Requires more computation

---

## Experiment Results

### Lung Cancer Classification

| Metric | Score |
|---|---:|
| Selected model | XGBoost |
| Accuracy | 0.8929 |
| Balanced accuracy | 0.7292 |
| Macro F1 | 0.7551 |
| ROC-AUC | 0.9583 |

The model performed strongly on the majority class but showed weaker recall for the minority class. This experiment demonstrated the importance of class-aware metrics instead of relying only on accuracy.

> This dataset is used only to demonstrate the AutoML workflow. The resulting model is not a medical diagnostic system.

### Insurance Charge Regression

| Metric | Score |
|---|---:|
| Selected model | XGBoost |
| Cross-validation RMSE | 4573.44 |
| Test MAE | 2501.01 |
| Test RMSE | 4287.35 |
| Test R² | 0.90 |

The model explained approximately 90% of the variation in insurance charges. The difference between MAE and RMSE suggests that some observations produced significantly larger errors than average.

---

## Generated Artifacts

Each AutoML execution creates a timestamped directory:

```text
automl_runs/
└── insurance_20260722_120000/
    ├── model_pipeline.joblib
    ├── target_encoder.joblib
    ├── input_schema.json
    ├── metadata.json
    ├── test_metrics.json
    ├── column_report.csv
    ├── baseline_leaderboard.csv
    ├── tuned_leaderboard.csv
    ├── classification_report.csv
    ├── prediction_preview.csv
    ├── baseline_failures.csv
    └── tuning_failures.csv
```

Some files are created only when relevant. For example, regression runs do not require a target encoder or classification report.

### Core deployment files

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `model_pipeline.joblib` | Complete preprocessing and trained model |
| `target_encoder.joblib` | Converts encoded classes back to original labels |
| `input_schema.json` | Defines expected prediction input |
| `metadata.json` | Stores model, dataset, and experiment information |

### Evaluation files

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `test_metrics.json` | Final held-out test performance |
| `baseline_leaderboard.csv` | Performance before tuning |
| `tuned_leaderboard.csv` | Performance after tuning |
| `classification_report.csv` | Per-class classification performance |
| `prediction_preview.csv` | Example actual and predicted values |

---

## Project Structure

```text
Auto-ML-Saas/
├── notebooks/
│   └── AutoML.ipynb
├── data/
├── automl_runs/
├── .gitignore
├── README.md
└── requirements.txt
```

The `data/` and `automl_runs/` directories are ignored by Git to avoid publishing datasets and large trained-model files.

